\details{
The detailed design of NEON plant survey can be found in \href{https://esajournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ecs2.2603}{Barnett et al. 2019}.
Here, we:
\enumerate{
\item Kept surveyed subplots only (\code{targetTaxaPresent} of \code{Y} or \code{N}; records flagged \code{samplingImpractical} have no taxa) and removed rows missing plotID, subplotID, eventID, or endDate.
\item Applied morphospecies resolutions from \code{div_morphospecies} (joined by \code{siteID} and \code{morphospeciesID}, NEON user guide sections 3.7 and 3.9), so field morphospecies later identified to genus or species carry the resolved \code{taxon_id}, \code{taxon_name}, and \code{taxon_rank}. Only unambiguous (one-to-one) resolutions were applied. Unresolved morphospecies that share a coarse code (e.g. \verb{2PLANT}) are kept as distinct taxa and can be told apart by \code{morphospeciesID}.
\item Where a subplot was surveyed on two dates within one sampling event (repeat surveys), kept the first survey. Where the same taxon was recorded twice in one subplot survey, kept the record with the highest percent cover.
\item Removed duplicate taxa between nested subplots (each taxon should be represented once per subplot per event). If a taxon is present in a 1 m² subplot, it is removed from the 10 m² subplot containing it; if present in any nested 1 or 10 m² subplot, it is removed from the 100 m² subplot.
\item Kept taxa identified to genus, speciesGroup, species, subspecies, or variety.
\item Standardized taxon names against the World Checklist of Vascular Plants (WCVP) by joining species-, subspecies-, and variety-level NEON names against a shared plant name base built with the \code{rWCVP} package (WCVP version recorded in \code{data-raw/plant_name_lookup.csv}). Specifically:
\itemize{
\item The join uses the full NEON name including authorship, because the same name can refer to different taxa depending on its author (homonyms).
\item In the name base, raw names are split into canonical name and authorship, matched exactly first and fuzzily only when needed, and synonyms are followed to the WCVP-accepted name. Fuzzy matches whose author contradicts the matched WCVP record, and names whose candidate matches disagree on the accepted taxon, are left unresolved rather than guessed.
\item The accepted names are stored in \code{accepted_wcvp_name} and \code{accepted_wcvp_name_binomial}; the original NEON name is retained in \code{taxon_name}.
\item Subspecies and varieties are matched as their own taxa, not as their species, even though NEON writes the species author before the rank (e.g. "Circaea lutetiana L. ssp. canadensis (L.) Asch. & Magnus" resolves to \emph{Circaea canadensis}).
\item Names with no accepted WCVP name (not in the name base, no match, or unresolved) and genus-level taxa retain their original NEON name, with one exception: autonyms (e.g. "Polygonum scandens L. var. scandens") are the species itself and take the accepted name of their NEON species.
\item Subspecies and varieties with no accepted WCVP name take the accepted binomial of their NEON species, so a species is not split between its accepted name and its old NEON name (e.g. \emph{Fallopia scandens} vs \emph{Polygonum scandens}).
\item WCVP treats \emph{Taraxacum officinale} as the aggregate "Taraxacum sect. Taraxacum". As in the shared plant name base, these records carry that name in both \code{accepted_wcvp_name} and \code{accepted_wcvp_name_binomial}; the original NEON name is kept in \code{taxon_name}.
\item speciesGroup taxa (groups of species that observers could not tell apart, e.g. "Fraxinus americana/pennsylvanica") were assigned to one member species in \code{accepted_wcvp_name} and \code{accepted_wcvp_name_binomial}: the first-listed member by default or, if it is not recorded as a species at the same site, the first later-listed member that is. Parts that name only a genus (e.g. "Andropogon L.") are never assigned. These records are flagged with \code{species_group_assigned = TRUE}; the original NEON name is kept in \code{taxon_name}.
}
\item Stacked species occurrence from different scales into a long data frame. A sampling event is identified by \code{plotID}, \code{boutNumber}, and the year of \code{observation_datetime} (or \code{unique_sample_id}, which combines them with the 100 m² subplot). Always include the event when building sample units, otherwise all years are pooled. Therefore,
\itemize{
\item to get the species list at 1 m² scale, we need all the data with \code{sample_area_m2 == 1} (e.g. \code{dplyr::filter(plants, sample_area_m2 == 1)}); the sample unit is \code{unique_sample_id} plus \code{subsubplot_id} (or \code{unique_sample_id} plus \code{subplotID}).
\item to get the species list at 10 m² scale, we need all the data with \code{sample_area_m2 <= 10}; the sample unit is \code{unique_sample_id} plus \code{subsubplot_id}.
\item to get the species list at 100 m² scale, we need use the whole data set since the maximum value of \code{sample_area_m2} is 100 (i.e. a 10 m by 10 m subplot); the sample unit is \code{unique_sample_id}.
\item to get the species list at 400 m² scale (i.e. one plot with four subplots), aggregate the data by \code{plotID}, \code{boutNumber}, and year.
}
}
}
\note{
Details of locations (e.g. latitude/longitude coordinates can be found in \link{neon_location}).

\strong{Zero rows.} A 1 m² subplot that was surveyed but contained no plant species is kept as one row with \code{taxon_id = NA}, \code{value = 0}, and \code{presence_absence = 0}. This zero-row convention is shared by all neonDivData observation datasets.

\strong{Sampling effort.} Since 2019, the 10 m² and 100 m² scales are surveyed only every other year (and in half of the plots in 2018), while 1 m² subplots are surveyed every year. Use \code{sampled_scales_m2} to restrict analyses to subplots where all scales were surveyed (\code{sampled_scales_m2 == "1,10,100"}) before comparing 100 m² or 400 m² species lists among plots or years.

\strong{speciesGroup records.} About 0.7\% of records are NEON speciesGroup taxa that were assigned to one member species in the WCVP name columns (\code{species_group_assigned = TRUE}). For most of these, the assigned species is the only member recorded at that site, or both members occur there, so the assignment does not add a spurious species. Use the assigned binomial for community-level analyses (e.g. richness, composition); drop flagged records for species-specific analyses of either member; or collapse them to genus for a conservative richness estimate.

\strong{Flags.} \code{redundant_genus} marks genus-level records that co-occur with a finer identification of the same genus in the same \code{unique_sample_id}; drop them (\code{!redundant_genus}) to avoid inflating species richness. \code{identificationQualifier} (e.g. "cf. species") marks uncertain identifications.
}
